[[dense-embeddings]]
Dense embeddings and semantic search

A dense embedding maps a chunk of text to a fixed-length vector of floating point
numbers so that passages with similar meaning land near each other in the vector
space. Retrieval then becomes a nearest-neighbour problem: embed the question once,
and return the stored vectors closest to it, usually measured by cosine similarity.

The strength of this approach is that it finds passages by intent rather than by
surface form. A search for "where is the login handled" can surface an authentication
routine that never uses the word "login", because the model has learned that the two
ideas are related. The weakness is the mirror image: similarity captures conceptual
closeness, not structural dependency, so it will not reliably connect a function to
the distant code that calls it.
